    "usageNote": "مَسْكَن belongs to the root س-ك-ن, whose basic sense is stillness and rest — a moving thing سَكَنَ (\"settled, stopped, calmed\") after motion. The verb extends to سَكَنَ الدَّارَ, \"he dwelt in the house,\" with verbal nouns سُكْنَى/سُكْنٌ; مَسْكَن is the place-noun built on that sense, so it names the physical place of dwelling — a house or abode, somewhere one \"comes to rest.\" Don't confuse it with the similar-looking مِسْكِين (a humble or needy person) — the same root entry notes سَكَنَ can also mean \"he became such as is termed miskīn,\" so the two words share a root but diverge in pattern and meaning."
